    <div class="case-study-body">
      <h4>The Challenge</h4>
      <p>This project was completed while studying UX design at General Assembly in 2014. Hangry tries to solve the problem of what to do when you're hungry and angry... Hangry?</p>
      <p>One of many class affinity diagram sessions. Elevator pitch sketch explaining my project.</p>

      <h4>Sketching, testing and prototyping</h4>
      <p>Throughout the project I conducted guerrilla testing with paper prototypes and user surveys.</p>
      <p><strong>Guerrilla testing</strong> &mdash; Conducted user testing with paper prototypes and the 'POP' app on the iPhone.</p>
      <p><strong>POP prototype</strong> &mdash; The POP app revealed some major usability issues that I rectified for further testing.</p>

      <h4>Design Studio Method</h4>
      <p>Conducted Design Studio Method testing with six participants. The question asked: What do you do when you're tired/angry and hungry &mdash; Hangry?</p>
      <p><strong>Round 1</strong> &mdash; Each participant presented their solutions to the group. They ranked their favourite ideas with red sticker dots.</p>
      <p><strong>Round 2</strong> &mdash; Further refinements were made then presented back to the group. The three standout ideas were chosen.</p>

      <h4>Personas</h4>
      <p><em>Persona detail to come.</em></p>

      <h4>Workshop — site map</h4>
      <p>Workshops were run with a diverse group of designers in the industry. I decided to map out the customer experience end to end.</p>

      <h4>Wireframes</h4>
      <p>After getting feedback on the site map I created low fidelity wireframes and built a prototype for user testing.</p>
